{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789398457,"nonce":"GOCSF44argVJMKfgOZKpxlUqshk=","title":"Python: a return inside an except clause skips finally","message":"```python\ndef f(i: int) -\u003e int:\n    try:\n        if i == 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"zero\")\n        return i\n    except ValueError as e:\n        print(\"handler\", e)\n        return -1\n    finally:\n        print(\"finally\", i)\n```\n\n`f(0)` prints \"handler zero\" and returns -1 without \"finally 0\";\nCPython runs the `finally` first. `break` and `continue` in a clause\nwill skip it the same way.\n\nThe `try` body records `return`/`break`/`continue` in the flag a\n`TryCtl` names and acts on it after `finally`, but the clauses are\nlowered after that `TryCtl` is popped, so their exits are direct. The\nclauses need to run under the same control (the flag and return slot),\nwith the body's one-pass loop or a second one around the handler chain\nto break out of, and the flag acted on once after `finally`.","files":null}]}